<description>
Flood-fill each marked chamber bounded by walls with its marker color.

Input structure
A same-size grid has a dominant background/open color, one repeated wall color,
and one-cell colored markers sitting inside some wall-bounded open chambers.

Rule
Infer the wall as the unique non-background color that appears more than once.
Treat wall pixels as barriers. For every 4-connected non-wall component, leave
it unchanged if it has no marker; otherwise assert it has exactly one marker and
recolor the whole component with that marker's color.

Key insight
The repeated color is a maze wall that partitions the background into chambers;
the singleton marker inside a chamber supplies the fill color for that chamber.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "4-connected non-wall regions made of background pixels and singleton colored markers, separated by the repeated wall color",
  "transform": "for each region containing a marker, recolor all non-wall pixels in that region to the marker color; leave unmarked regions and wall pixels unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 7-9 rows x 7-9 cols (square)
  colors:
    background: 0 (black), most frequent color (~55-60% of cells)
    wall: 5 (gray), second most frequent (~39-44% of cells), appears >1 time
    markers: 2-3 distinct singleton colors (each appears exactly once), not 0 or 5
  wall_structure:
    scattered individual cells and small clusters (not a contiguous 4-connected mass)
    nearly all cells form one 8-connected component [observed]
    acts as 4-connected barrier partitioning bg into chambers
    present on grid border (8-12 wall cells touch border) [observed]
  chambers:
    marked: 2-3 chambers each containing exactly 1 marker cell, size 3-13
    unmarked: 5-8 small regions with no marker, size 1-10
    all marked chambers touch the grid border [observed]
  output_rule: >
    flood-fill each marked chamber (4-connected non-wall region containing a marker)
    with that marker's color; leave unmarked regions and wall cells unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size 7x7 or 9x9; pick 2-3 marker colors from {1,2,3,4,6,7,8,9}
    2. Build output by partitioning grid into regions with scattered wall cells (color 5)
       occupying ~40% of cells; ensure 4-connected non-wall regions include 2-3 large
       chambers (size 3-13) each fully filled with one marker color, plus several small
       unmarked bg regions
    3. Validate: each marked chamber is 4-connected, exactly one marker color per chamber,
       wall nearly 8-connected, marked chambers touch border
    4. Derive input from output: replace all filled marker-color cells in each marked
       chamber with bg (0) except one randomly chosen cell (the marker seed)
